{"success": true, "message": "Test details sent successfully", "data": {"test": {"_id": "69de21f27e39d99b57bc5b29", "analysisVideoCdn": "yt", "exam": ["605dfcb2f226ac25a8bd5fef"], "ctype": null, "position": 0, "order": 12, "enableCalculator": false, "isContinuousQuestion": false, "reattemptAvailable": false, "singleSectionMultipleSubjects": false, "timeRestricted": false, "title": "Structure of Atom - Test 4", "description": "Previous Year Questions as Chapter Test", "syllabus": "This test contains questions from all topics of this chapter included in the JEE Main syllabus. It includes a mix of questions from PYQs of JEE Main 2019 - 2025.", "totalTime": 37, "totalQuestions": 25, "totalMarks": 100, "bonusQuestions": null, "bonusMarks": null, "startDate": "2026-04-15T03:30:00.000Z", "endDate": "2027-04-30T00:00:00.000Z", "type": "free", "class": "605df635a37b7025c438d685", "instruction": "608289ffb160b022e9442c83", "subjects": [{"subjectId": "607018ee404ae53194e73d91", "totalMarks": 100, "bonusMarks": 0, "totalQuestions": 25, "bonusQuestions": 0, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261d8d"}], "sections": [{"sectionId": {"_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261bff", "title": "Chemistry Single Correct", "sectionType": "normal", "subject": "607018ee404ae53194e73d91", "questions": [{"_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a61", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f49"], "concepts": ["60e2fb5666f9ce3f243b4f27"], "question": {"text": "If the radius of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>3</mn><mi>nd</mi></msup></math> Bohr&#39;s orbit of hydrogen atom is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> and the radius of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>th</mi></msup></math> Bohr&#39;s orbit is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>. Then"}, "options": [{"id": "96d5b7", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>9</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a62"}, {"id": "8b9328", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>16</mn><mn>9</mn></mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a63"}, {"id": "6d4a90", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a64"}, {"id": "0fa923", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a65"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a66", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fb2066f9ce3f243b4b78"], "concepts": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b42"], "question": {"text": "<p>Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R :</p><p>Assertion A: In the photoelectric effect, the electrons are ejected from the metal surface as soon as the beam of light of frequency greater than threshold frequency strikes the surface.</p><p>Reason R : When the photon of any energy strikes an electron in the atom, transfer of energy from the photon to the electron takes place.</p><p>In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "367cca", "text": "Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a67"}, {"id": "c4684a", "text": "A is correct but R is not correct", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a68"}, {"id": "c81add", "text": "Both A and R are correct but R is NOT the correct explanation of A", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a69"}, {"id": "ac5b03", "text": "A is not correct but R is correct", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a6a"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a6b", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b478d"], "concepts": ["60e5678c499d1e49588f9268"], "question": {"text": "In case of isoelectronic species the size of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mo>–</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><mi>Ne</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Na</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math> is affected by:"}, "options": [{"id": "91501f", "text": "Principal quantum number <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a6c"}, {"id": "106432", "text": "None of the factors because their size is the same", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a6d"}, {"id": "dbc750", "text": "Electron-electron interaction in the outer orbitals", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a6e"}, {"id": "836f2b", "text": "Nuclear charge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a6f"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a70", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e5671c499d1e49588f8801"], "concepts": ["60e5671a499d1e49588f87da"], "question": {"text": "Among the following, the energy of <math><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math> orbital is lowest in:"}, "options": [{"id": "df73d9", "text": "<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a71"}, {"id": "8e3da4", "text": "<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a72"}, {"id": "03e06c", "text": "<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a73"}, {"id": "c93e6e", "text": "<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a74"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a75", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b477c"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4730"], "question": {"text": "<p>For any given series of spectral lines of atomic hydrogen, let&nbsp; <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#916;</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v</mi></mrow><mo>-</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v</mi></mrow><mo>-</mo></mover></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v</mi></mrow><mo>-</mo></mover></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></mrow></msub></math>&nbsp;&nbsp;be the difference in maximum and minimum&nbsp;wave number&nbsp;in <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>.&nbsp;</p><p>The ratio <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#916;</mi><msub><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v</mi></mrow><mo>-</mo></mover></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#916;</mi><msub><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v</mi></mrow><mo>-</mo></mover></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></msub></math> is</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "6abdfc", "text": "<math><mn>5</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>4</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a76"}, {"id": "992ef9", "text": "<math><mn>27</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>5</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a77"}, {"id": "a26e07", "text": "<math><mn>4</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a78"}, {"id": "14c467", "text": "<math><mn>9</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>4</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a79"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a7a", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b478d"], "concepts": ["60e56768499d1e49588f8eee"], "question": {"text": "A certain orbital has no angular nodes and two radial nodes. The orbital is:"}, "options": [{"id": "cde5ec", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a7b"}, {"id": "3c4eb9", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a7c"}, {"id": "7d307f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a7d"}, {"id": "5edfcb", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a7e"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a7f", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fb2066f9ce3f243b4b78"], "concepts": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b5671"], "question": {"text": "The de Broglie wavelength of an electron in the <math><msup><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></msup></math> Bohr orbit is:"}, "options": [{"id": "c1124d", "text": "<math><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a80"}, {"id": "03fb0d", "text": "<math><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a81"}, {"id": "aadf86", "text": "<math><mn>6</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a82"}, {"id": "5fc12e", "text": "<math><mn>8</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a83"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a84", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f49"], "concepts": ["6162fb756ed13040ec84f256"], "question": {"text": "<p>Given below are two statements:</p><p>Statement I : Bohr&#39;s theory accounts for the stability and line spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Li</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math>&nbsp; ion.</p><p>Statement II : Bohr&#39;s theory was unable to explain the splitting of spectral lines in the presence of a magnetic field.</p><p>In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "1dbe91", "text": "Both statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math> and statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>II</mi></math> are true.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a85"}, {"id": "047571", "text": "Statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math> is false but statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>II</mi></math> is true.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a86"}, {"id": "fd528b", "text": "Both statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math> and statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>II</mi></math> are false.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a87"}, {"id": "e3134e", "text": "<p>Statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math> is true but statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>II</mi></math> is false.</p>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a88"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a89", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e5671c499d1e49588f8801"], "concepts": ["60e5671a499d1e49588f87da"], "question": {"text": "<p>Given below are two statements. One is labelled as Assertion <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> and the other is labelled as Reason <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math>.</p><p>Assertion <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> : Energy of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>&nbsp;orbital of hydrogen atom is greater than that of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math> orbital of lithium.</p><p>Reason <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math> : Energies of the orbitals in the same subshell decrease with increase in the atomic number.</p><p>In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "c0ce6a", "text": "Both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math> are true and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math> is the correct explanation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a8a"}, {"id": "efa965", "text": "Both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math> are true but <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math> is NOT the correct explanation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a8b"}, {"id": "cd2a8f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> is true but <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math> is false.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a8c"}, {"id": "af10e3", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>&nbsp;is false but <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math> is true.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a8d"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a8e", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b477c"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4730"], "question": {"text": "The ratio of the shortest wavelength of two spectral series of hydrogen spectrum is found to be about <math><mn>9</mn></math>. The spectral series are:"}, "options": [{"id": "bc497d", "text": "Paschen and Pfund", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a8f"}, {"id": "5b15e7", "text": "Balmer and Brackett", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a90"}, {"id": "fd1c0a", "text": "Lyman and Paschen", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a91"}, {"id": "18981e", "text": "Brackett and Pfund", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a92"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a93", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b478d"], "concepts": ["60e5671a499d1e49588f87f1"], "question": {"text": "Given below are two statements :\nStatement (I) : For a given shell, the total number of allowed orbitals is given by $\\mathrm{n}^2$.\nStatement (II) : For any subshell, the spatial orientation of the orbitals is given by $-l$ to $+l$ values including zero.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :"}, "options": [{"id": "367bc2", "text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are false", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a94"}, {"id": "26058a", "text": "Statement I is true but Statement II is false", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a95"}, {"id": "3b2d5d", "text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are true", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a96"}, {"id": "5a4ce8", "text": "Statement I is false but Statement II is true", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a97"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a98", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f49"], "concepts": ["60e2fb5666f9ce3f243b4f27"], "question": {"text": "The radius of the 2<sup>nd</sup> orbit of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Li</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></math>. The expected radius of the 3<sup>rd</sup> orbit of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Be</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>&nbsp;is"}, "options": [{"id": "1259f2", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>9</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a99"}, {"id": "68c7bb", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>9</mn></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a9a"}, {"id": "7daefa", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>27</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a9b"}, {"id": "072bc9", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>16</mn><mn>27</mn></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a9c"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a9d", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fb2066f9ce3f243b4b78"], "concepts": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b42"], "question": {"text": "<p>If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></math> is the momentum of the fastest electron ejected from a metal surface after the irradiation of light having wavelength <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#955;</mi><mo>,</mo></math> then for <math><mn>1.5</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></math> momentum of the photoelectron, the wavelength of the light should be:</p><p>(Assume kinetic energy of ejected photoelectron to be very high in comparison to work function)</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "128491", "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">λ</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a9e"}, {"id": "bfa396", "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">λ</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261a9f"}, {"id": "d9e1d8", "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">λ</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261aa0"}, {"id": "525620", "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">λ</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261aa1"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261aa2", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e5671c499d1e49588f8801"], "concepts": ["60e5671a499d1e49588f87da"], "question": {"text": "The electronic configuration for Neodymium is:</p><p>[Atomic Number for Neodymium 60]"}, "options": [{"id": "ac9d6b", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>[</mo><mi>Xe</mi><mo>]</mo><mo> </mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo> </mo><mn>6</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261aa3"}, {"id": "8f0edc", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>[</mo><mi>Xe</mi><mo>]</mo><mo> </mo><mn>5</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mn>7</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261aa4"}, {"id": "a81bc9", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>[</mo><mi>Xe</mi><mo>]</mo><mo> </mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo> </mo><mn>6</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261aa5"}, {"id": "01369e", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>[</mo><mi>Xe</mi><mo>]</mo><mo> </mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mn>5</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mn>6</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261aa6"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261aa7", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b478d"], "concepts": ["6117bbff67dbe2603c3ec24d"], "question": {"text": "Which of the following postulate of Bohr's model of hydrogen atom in not in agreement with quantum mechanical model of an atom ?"}, "options": [{"id": "6c9bd8", "text": "An atom in a stationary state does not emit electromagnetic radiation as long as it stays in the same state", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261aa8"}, {"id": "2cb819", "text": "An atom can take only certain distinct energies $\\mathrm{E}_1, \\mathrm{E}_2, \\mathrm{E}_3$, etc. These allowed states of constant energy are called the stationary states of atom", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261aa9"}, {"id": "122266", "text": "When an electron makes a transition from a higher energy stationary state to a lower energy stationary state, then it emits a photon of light", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261aaa"}, {"id": "b29ee9", "text": "The electron in a H atom's stationary state moves in a circle around the nucleus", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261aab"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261aac", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f49"], "concepts": ["60e2fb5666f9ce3f243b4f27"], "question": {"text": "Radius of the first excited state of Helium ion is given as : $\\mathrm{a}_0 \\rightarrow$ radius of first stationary state of hydrogen atom."}, "options": [{"id": "ac1998", "text": "$\\mathrm{r}=4 \\mathrm{a}_0$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261aad"}, {"id": "77804c", "text": "$\\mathrm{r}=2 \\mathrm{a}_0$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261aae"}, {"id": "d5d3de", "text": "$r=\\frac{a_0}{2}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261aaf"}, {"id": "7a1c34", "text": "$r=\\frac{a_0}{4}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261ab0"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261ab1", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e5671c499d1e49588f8801"], "concepts": ["60e5671a499d1e49588f87da"], "question": {"text": "Consider the hypothetical situation where the azimuthal quantum number, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"script\">l</mi></math>, takes values <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8230;</mo><mo>&#8230;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn></math>.&nbsp;Where n is the principal quantum number. Then, the element with atomic number:"}, "options": [{"id": "f635ac", "text": "9 is the first alkali metal", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261ab2"}, {"id": "aeb397", "text": "13 has a half-filled valence subshell", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261ab3"}, {"id": "71112b", "text": "8 is the first noble gas", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261ab4"}, {"id": "92e36b", "text": "6 has a 2p-valence subshell", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261ab5"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261ab6", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b478d"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4746"], "question": {"text": "Which of the following electronic configuration would be associated with the highest magnetic moment ?"}, "options": [{"id": "c8c151", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>[</mo><mi>Ar</mi><mo>]</mo><mo> </mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mn>7</mn></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261ab7"}, {"id": "25d41a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>[</mo><mi>Ar</mi><mo>]</mo><mo> </mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mn>8</mn></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261ab8"}, {"id": "a8f427", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>[</mo><mi>Ar</mi><mo>]</mo><mo> </mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261ab9"}, {"id": "d9e6bb", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>[</mo><mi>Ar</mi><mo>]</mo><mo> </mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261aba"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f482ee0e063d9261abb", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb1"], "topics": ["60e5671c499d1e49588f8801"], "concepts": ["60e5671a499d1e49588f87da"], "question": {"text": "<p>The correct decreasing order of energy, for the orbitals having, following set of quantum numbers:</p><p>(A)&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal